---
import Adr from "../../components/Adr.astro";
import Layer from "../../components/Layer.astro";
import Layout from "../../components/Layout.astro";
import PageLink from "../../components/PageLink.astro";
import Path from "../../components/Path.astro";
import Rule from "../../components/Rule.astro";
import Sequence from "../../components/Sequence.astro";

const S = "server/src/iki_server";
const H = {
  short: "Postgres carries both",
  how: "From a commit to a task and a page",
  rules: "Tasks, webhooks and streams",
  ref: "Files and decisions",
};
const toc = [
  { id: "short", label: H.short },
  { id: "how", label: H.how },
  { id: "rules", label: H.rules },
  { id: "ref", label: H.ref },
];
---

<Layout toc={toc}>
  <Layer n={1} title={H.short}>
    <p>
      Background work is Django's Tasks framework on a Postgres backend. A task's row is written in
      the transaction of the change that asks for it, and <code>NOTIFY iki_tasks</code> wakes a
      runner when that transaction commits; runners claim rows with <code>FOR UPDATE SKIP
      LOCKED</code>, so any number share the queue. Live updates also go through Postgres: a change
      calls <code>pg_notify('iki_events', …)</code>, each web process holds one <code>LISTEN</code>{" "}
      connection, and it fans each event out to its open Server-Sent Events streams. Events carry
      identifiers, never content: the page reads what changed through the API.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>Postgres is the only stateful service: no Redis, no RabbitMQ, no Celery (<Adr n={[5, 30]} />).</li>
      <li>A rolled-back change runs no tasks and sends no events.</li>
      <li>Tasks run at least once, so every task must be idempotent.</li>
      <li>Webhooks are sent by the task runner, signed (<Adr n="0031" />).</li>
    </ul>
  </Layer>

  <Layer n={2} title={H.how}>
    <p>
      What one change sets off, when it asks for background work and has something to tell open
      pages. The job queue for runs works the same way on its own channel (<PageLink
        to="The worker and its leases"
      />).
    </p>
    <Sequence
      id="tasks-seq"
      title="A change, its task and its live update"
      width={880}
      actors={[
        { id: "web", label: "Web pod", sub: "a request" },
        { id: "db", label: "Postgres" },
        { id: "runner", label: "Task runner", sub: "iki-server tasks" },
        { id: "hub", label: "Hub", sub: "in each web pod" },
        { id: "tab", label: "Browser tab", sub: "SSE stream" },
      ]}
      steps={[
        { id: "write", from: "web", to: "db", label: "the change, a task row, NOTIFY, pg_notify" },
        { id: "commit", from: "db", to: "db", label: "commit: notifications go out now, or never" },
        { phase: "Background work" },
        { id: "wake", from: "db", to: "runner", label: "iki_tasks wakes an idle thread" },
        { id: "claim", from: "runner", to: "db", label: "claim: SKIP LOCKED, a lease and a token" },
        { id: "finish", from: "runner", to: "db", label: "record the outcome, if the token still holds" },
        { phase: "Live update" },
        { id: "listen", from: "db", to: "hub", label: "iki_events on the one LISTEN connection" },
        { id: "fan", from: "hub", to: "tab", label: "to each stream following its topic, once checked" },
        { id: "read", from: "tab", to: "web", label: "the page reads what changed through /api/v1", dashed: true },
      ]}
    >
      <div slot="write">
        <p>
          <code>enqueue()</code> writes to <code>iki_task</code> in the caller's transaction; it must
          not be wrapped in <code>on_commit</code>. <code>publish_to()</code> refuses a payload over
          7,000 bytes: Postgres refuses 8,000.
        </p>
        <p><Path p={`${S}/tasks/backend.py`} sym="class PostgresBackend" />, <Path p={`${S}/realtime/events.py`} sym="def publish_to(" /></p>
      </div>
      <div slot="wake">
        <p>Runners also poll every 5 seconds, so a missed notification only delays a task.</p>
        <p><Path p={`${S}/tasks/runner.py`} sym="POLL_EVERY" /></p>
      </div>
      <div slot="claim">
        <p>
          The highest priority ready task, then the oldest. The claim is a 60-second lease, renewed
          while the task runs, with a new fencing token each time it's claimed.
        </p>
        <p><Path p={`${S}/tasks/runner.py`} sym="def claim(" /></p>
      </div>
      <div slot="finish">
        <p>
          A runner presumed dead that comes back can't overwrite the attempt that replaced it.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div slot="listen">
        <p>
          A dedicated connection, not a pooled one. If it drops, the hub reconnects with backoff and
          tells every stream to <code>resync</code>.
        </p>
        <p><Path p={`${S}/realtime/hub.py`} sym="class Hub" /></p>
      </div>
      <div slot="fan">
        <p>
          A topic is a person's ID, or a thing many may follow such as <code>run:&lt;id&gt;</code>.
          Which events reach whom: <PageLink to="What events disclose" />.
        </p>
        <p><Path p={`${S}/realtime/views.py`} sym="async def events(" /></p>
      </div>
    </Sequence>
  </Layer>

  <Layer n={4} title={H.rules}>
    <div class="rules">
      <Rule title="Retries and failures" open>
        <p>
          A task declared with <code>max_attempts</code> is retried with exponential backoff: 30
          seconds, doubling, at most an hour, with ±20% jitter. Raising <code>Retry</code> chooses
          the delay; <code>GiveUp</code> fails at once. An attempt whose runner stopped renewing its
          lease goes back to the queue (<Path p={`${S}/tasks/runner.py`} sym="def backoff(" />).{" "}
          <code>maintain</code> deletes finished tasks after a week and failed ones after 30 days.
        </p>
      </Rule>
      <Rule title="The runner's housekeeping">
        <p>
          Every 15 seconds each runner renews its leases and returns expired attempts. The same
          thread ends jobs whose workers went away and setup builds' likewise, moves on workflow runs
          nothing moved on, fires scheduled triggers, recovers bulk and spreadsheet imports, and
          sends budget alerts, so any runner can do it and none is special (<Path
            p={`${S}/tasks/runner.py`}
            sym="def _housekeep("
          />). Those mechanisms are on <PageLink to="The worker and its leases" /> and <PageLink
            to="Workflows and triggers"
          />.
        </p>
      </Rule>
      <Rule title="Webhooks">
        <p>
          A delivery is a task, retried on the Standard Webhooks schedule: eight attempts over
          about 28 hours (<Path p={`${S}/webhooks/tasks.py`} sym="SCHEDULE" />). Each message is
          signed with HMAC-SHA256 over its ID, timestamp and body, and carries two signatures while
          a secret rotates (<Path p={`${S}/webhooks/signing.py`} />). The sender resolves the host
          once and connects only to public addresses, unless the operator allowed a network for
          receivers inside it; it keeps TLS on the real name, follows no
          redirects, and keeps only the status code (<Path p={`${S}/webhooks/sender.py`} />, <Adr
            n="0031"
          />).
        </p>
      </Rule>
      <Rule title="Live-update streams">
        <p>
          <code>GET /api/v1/events</code> is one stream per tab, with the session or a bearer token
          with <code>read</code> scope; runs, batches, workflow runs and setup builds have streams of
          their own. The person's stream starts with <code>ready</code>, the unread notification
          count. A stream sends a comment every 20 seconds so
          proxies keep it open, and ends after about 30 minutes or at shutdown; browsers reconnect by
          themselves. Before each event and at each comment it checks the session or token again,
          ending with <code>signed_out</code> when it's no longer valid (<Path
            p={`${S}/realtime/views.py`}
            sym="MAX_LIFETIME"
          />).
        </p>
        <p>
          A stream holds no database connection while it waits. A stream that falls 100 events
          behind is emptied and sent <code>resync</code>, so a stuck client refetches rather than
          holding memory. A web process holds at most 2,000 streams, 20 per person.
        </p>
      </Rule>
    </div>
  </Layer>

  <Layer n={5} title={H.ref}>
    <div class="refs">
      <div>
        <h3>Files</h3>
        <ul>
          <li><Path p={`${S}/tasks`} /> the backend, runner and <code>tasks</code> command</li>
          <li><Path p={`${S}/realtime`} /> publishing, the hub, the stream views</li>
          <li><Path p={`${S}/webhooks`} /> events, signing, sending</li>
          <li><Path p="deploy/helm/iki/templates/tasks-deployment.yaml" /></li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h3>Decisions</h3>
        <ul>
          <li><Adr n="0005" title /></li>
          <li><Adr n="0030" title /></li>
          <li><Adr n="0031" title /></li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
  </Layer>
</Layout>
